<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bihar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bihar is a state of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. In antiquity it was the land of Videha, the Vajji confederacy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> served the Maurya and Gupta empires, and the Buddha attained enlightenment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>. Its name comes from its many Buddhist monasteries (viharas), and it later belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bihar.jpg" alt="Bihar" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Odantapuribs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_No.-_3,_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bihar borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> to the north, the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> to the south, and covers 94,163 square kilometres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> flows west to east through the state and divides its fertile plain into North Bihar and South Bihar. Along with its tributaries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandaki-river">Gandak</a> and Koshi from the Nepalese Himalayas and the Bagmati, it regularly floods parts of the plain. The name is generally believed to derive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Pali vihāra, a monastery or religious dwelling, and medieval Persian and Turkic writers applied it to the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chirand, on the northern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in Saran district, has an archaeological record from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, about 2500 to 1345 BCE. Regions of Bihar such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> and Anga are mentioned in the religious texts and epics of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. According to Buddhist tradition the Buddha attained enlightenment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, at the Mahabodhi Temple. Early Buddhist centres of learning such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a> arose in Bihar and drew students and scholars from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and under dynasties including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> and Guptas Buddhism received royal patronage and spread from the region to other parts of Asia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> declined after the invasion of Muhammad bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>, when many viharas were destroyed together with the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a>. Some historians believe thousands of Buddhist monks were massacred in the 12th century, while D. N. Jha suggests that these incidents resulted from Buddhist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> skirmishes in a fight for supremacy. After the Pala Empire the Karnat dynasty came to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> in the 11th century, and the Oiniwar dynasty succeeded it in the 14th.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764 the British East India Company obtained the diwani rights, the right to administer and collect revenue, for Bihar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. Bihar remained part of the Bengal Presidency until 1912, when Bihar and Orissa were carved out as a new province. In April 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> visited Champaran, where Raj Kumar Shukla had drawn his attention to the exploitation of the peasants by European indigo planters, and the Champaran Satyagraha that followed drew support from Bihari nationalists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bihar is a state of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Mahabodhi Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Its official languages are Hindi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, and the main native languages are Maithili, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magahi">Magahi</a> and Bhojpuri.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bihar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Clive's victory at the Battle of Plassey in 1757 deposed the Nawab of Bengal Siraj-ud-Daulah in favour of Mir Jafar, and the Battle of Buxar in 1764 forced the emperor Shah Alam II to appoint the Company diwan, or revenue collector, of Bengal, <b>Bihar</b> and Orissa in 1765.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1194 he defeated the Gahadavala king Jayachandra at the Battle of Chandawar, and his commanders went on to raid as far as <b>Bihar</b> and Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Mihira Bhoja (r. c. 836–885) and Mahendrapala I (r. c. 885–910) the Pratiharas gained the upper hand, extending their rule from Punjab and Gujarat to <b>Bihar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Internal conflict followed: war with Koch <b>Bihar</b> in 1711, a Tibetan attack in 1714, the British East India Company's ousting of the Bhutanese from Koch Bihar in 1772 and attack on Bhutan in 1774, a century of skirmishes and the Duar War (1864–65), after which the Treaty of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Magadha lay south of the Ganges in what is now southern <b>Bihar</b>, a land of alluvial plains bounded by the river Son to the west and the forested hills of the Chota Nagpur plateau to the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He extended Pala rule over <b>Bihar</b>, pushed into the Ganges valley and, after setbacks against the Gurjara-Pratihara king Vatsaraja and the Rashtrakuta Dhruva, installed his own candidate, Chakrayudha, on the throne of Kannauj.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
